This project (EE-456: RF & mm-Wave Active Circuits) designs a **15 GHz low-noise amplifier (LNA) matching network** for the **MGF4941AL InGaAs HEMT** at **VDS = 2 V** and **IDS = 10 mA**. Unlike Project 1 (pure max-gain), the goal here is a **practical compromise** that meets **gain + noise + matching** specifications simultaneously, while validating results in **both MATLAB and Keysight ADS**.

**Course:** EE-456 Microwave Active Circuits
**Assignment:** Design Project 02
**Toolchain:** MATLAB + Keysight ADS (ideal transmission-line matching + Touchstone verification)  
**Center frequency:** f0 = 15 GHz (swept ~14–16 GHz)

---

## Design Specs (Course Targets @ f0)

- **Transducer gain:** \(G_T \ge 12\ \text{dB}\)
- **Noise figure:** \(NF \le 0.6\ \text{dB}\)
- **Matching constraint:** \(VSWR \le 1.5\) (input and output networks)

---

## Strategy: MAG vs Fmin vs “Meets Everything” (Design 2)

The design begins with device S-parameters and noise parameters at **15 GHz**, then uses Smith-chart geometry to pick a termination that satisfies the constraints.

- **MAG design:** maximizes gain, but noise figure is too high (fails NF spec).
- **Fmin design:** hits minimum noise figure, but gain falls short (fails gain spec).
- **Design 2 (final):** selects \(\Gamma_S\) and \(\Gamma_L\) from the feasible region defined by **noise figure circles**, **available gain circles**, and **stability constraints**, then realizes the terminations with **single-stub transmission-line networks**.

